solution:
Blue vitriol is the pentahydrate of copper(II) sulfate, so its chemical formula is  

\[
\mathrm{CuSO_4\cdot5H_2O}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{\mathrm{CuSO_4\cdot5H_2O}}

Vanillin has the molecular formula \( \mathrm{C_8H_8O_3} \).  
The subscripts share no common divisor greater than 1, so the empirical formula is identical to the molecular formula.

Final answer: \boxed{\mathrm{C_8H_8O_3}}

At STP 1 mol of an ideal gas occupies 22.4 L.  

\[
n=\frac{V}{22.4\ \text{L mol}^{-1}}=\frac{75.8\ \text{L}}{22.4\ \text{L mol}^{-1}}\approx3.38\ \text{mol}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{3.38\ \text{mol}}

The photosynthetic synthesis of glucose follows the balanced equation  

\[
6\,\mathrm{CO_2}+6\,\mathrm{H_2O}\;\longrightarrow\;\mathrm{C_6H_{12}O_6}+6\,\mathrm{O_2}
\]

Thus, six carbon dioxide molecules supply the six carbon atoms required for one molecule of glucose.

Final answer: \boxed{6}

The balanced equation shows 3 mol of Fe producing 1 mol of Fe₃O₄. Hence the mole ratio  

\[
\frac{\text{Fe}_3\text{O}_4}{\text{Fe}} = \frac{1}{3}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{\frac{1}{3}}

The reaction combines diphosphorus pentoxide with water to form phosphoric acid. Balancing phosphorus, oxygen and hydrogen gives  

\[
\mathrm{P_2O_5 + 3\,H_2O \;\longrightarrow\; 2\,H_3PO_4}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{P_2O_5 + 3H_2O \rightarrow 2H_3PO_4}

Using the ideal‑gas relation \(P V/T =\) constant,
\[
P_2 = P_1\frac{V_1}{V_2}\frac{T_2}{T_1}
      =1.81\;\text{atm}\times\frac{5.00\ \text{L}}{25.0\ \text{L}}
        \times\frac{298\ \text{K}}{308\ \text{K}}
      \approx 0.351\ \text{atm}.
\]

Final answer: \boxed{0.351\ \text{atm}}

Molarity \(M\) is defined as moles of solute divided by volume of solution in liters:

\[
M = \frac{n}{V}
\]

Given \(n = 0.75\ \text{mol}\) and \(V = 500.0\ \text{mL}=0.500\ \text{L}\),

\[
M = \frac{0.75\ \text{mol}}{0.500\ \text{L}} = 1.5\ \text{M}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{1.5\ \text{M}}

The specific heat \(c\) is found from \(Q = mc\Delta T\).

\[
c = \frac{Q}{m\Delta T}
      = \frac{41.72\ \text{J}}{(18.69\ \text{g})(27.0-10.0)^\circ\text{C}}
\]

Convert the mass to kilograms: \(18.69\ \text{g}=0.01869\ \text{kg}\); \(\Delta T = 17.0\ \text{K}\).

\[
c = \frac{41.72}{0.01869 \times 17.0}
    = \frac{41.72}{0.31773}
    \approx 1.31 \times 10^{2}\ \text{J/(kg·K)}
\]

Thus the specific heat of the gold is about \(1.3 \times 10^{2}\ \text{J/(kg·K)}\).

Final answer: \boxed{1.31\times10^{2}\ \text{J/(kg·K)}}

The volume \(V\) follows from \(V = \frac{n}{c}\).

\[
V = \frac{1.90\ \text{mol}}{0.555\ \text{mol·L}^{-1}} = 3.42\ \text{L}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{3.42\ \text{L}}

The specific heat \(c\) follows \(Q = m\,c\,\Delta T\).

\[
c = \frac{Q}{m\Delta T}= \frac{1560\ \text{cal}}{312\ \text{g}\times 15^\circ\text{C}}
    = \frac{1560}{4680}\ \text{cal/(g·°C)}
    = 0.333\ \text{cal/(g·°C)}.
\]

Final answer: \boxed{0.333\ \text{cal/(g·°C)}}

\[
K_w=[\mathrm{H_3O^+}][\mathrm{OH^-}]=1.0\times10^{-14}
\]

\[
[\mathrm{OH^-}] = \frac{K_w}{[\mathrm{H_3O^+}]}
               = \frac{1.0\times10^{-14}}{5.36\times10^{-5}}
               = \frac{1}{5.36}\times10^{-9}
               \approx 1.87\times10^{-10}\ \text{M}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{1.87\times10^{-10}}

Using \(C_1V_1=C_2V_2\):

\[
V_2=\frac{C_1V_1}{C_2}= \frac{11\ \text{M}\times55\ \text{mL}}{0.137\ \text{M}}
       =\frac{605}{0.137}\text{ mL}\approx4.42\times10^{3}\text{ mL}
       =4.42\ \text{L}.
\]

Thus you must dilute to about **4.42 L** total volume.

Final answer: \boxed{4.42\ \text{L}}

At STP the total pressure of the gas mixture equals the standard atmospheric pressure, \(P_{\text{total}} = 101.3\;\text{kPa}\). The partial pressures add:

\[
P_{\text{C}} = P_{\text{total}}-P_{\text{A}}-P_{\text{B}}
           = 101.3\;\text{kPa} - 38.0\;\text{kPa} - 19.0\;\text{kPa}
           = 44.3\;\text{kPa}.
\]

Final answer: \boxed{44.3\ \text{kPa}}

Zn(OH)₂ provides two hydroxide ions, each neutralized by one acetic‑acid proton, forming water and acetate ions that coordinate Zn²⁺. Thus two moles of CH₃COOH are required per mole of Zn(OH)₂.

Balanced equation:
\[
\mathrm{Zn(OH)_2 + 2\,CH_3COOH \;\longrightarrow\; Zn(CH_3COO)_2 + 2\,H_2O}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{\mathrm{Zn(OH)_2 + 2CH_3COOH \rightarrow Zn(CH_3COO)_2 + 2H_2O}}

The decomposition of potassium chlorate is balanced by placing coefficients that equalize each element on both sides:

\[
2\,\text{KClO}_{3} \;\longrightarrow\; 2\,\text{KCl} \;+\; 3\,\text{O}_{2}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{2\,\text{KClO}_{3} \rightarrow 2\,\text{KCl} + 3\,\text{O}_{2}}

The equation is balanced by placing a coefficient of 2 in front of H₂ and H₂O, leaving O₂ unchanged:

\[2\text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow 2\text{H}_2\text{O}\]

Final answer: \boxed{2\ \text{H}_2 + \text{O}_2 \rightarrow 2\ \text{H}_2\text{O}}

In acidic solution the chlorate ion is reduced from Cl (+5) to Cl‑ (–1) while hydrazine is oxidized from N (–2) in N₂H₄ to N (+2) in NO.  

**Reduction half‑reaction**

\[
\mathrm{ClO_3^- + 6\,H^+ + 6e^- \;\longrightarrow\; Cl^- + 3\,H_2O}
\]

**Oxidation half‑reaction**

\[
\mathrm{N_2H_4 + 2\,H_2O \;\longrightarrow\; 2\,NO + 8\,H^+ + 8e^-}
\]

To equalise electrons multiply the reduction by 4 and the oxidation by 3 (24 e⁻ each) and add:

\[
\begin{aligned}
4\mathrm{ClO_3^-}+24\mathrm{H^+}+3\mathrm{N_2H_4}+6\mathrm{H_2O}
&\rightarrow 4\mathrm{Cl^-}+12\mathrm{H_2O}+6\mathrm{NO}+24\mathrm{H^+}
\end{aligned}
\]

Cancel the common \(\mathrm{H^+}\) and \(\mathrm{H_2O}\) terms:

\[
\boxed{4\mathrm{ClO_3^-}+3\mathrm{N_2H_4}\;\longrightarrow\;4\mathrm{Cl^-}+6\mathrm{NO}+6\mathrm{H_2O}}
\]

Thus chlorate oxidises hydrazine, producing nitric oxide and chloride ion.  

Final answer: \boxed{4\mathrm{ClO_3^-}+3\mathrm{N_2H_4}\rightarrow4\mathrm{Cl^-}+6\mathrm{NO}+6\mathrm{H_2O}}

The dissolution is  

\[
\text{SrSO}_4(s)\rightleftharpoons \text{Sr}^{2+}+ \text{SO}_4^{2-}
\]

so \(K_{sp}= [\text{Sr}^{2+}][\text{SO}_4^{2-}]=s^2\).  

\[
s=\sqrt{K_{sp}}=\sqrt{2.8\times10^{-7}}
      =\sqrt{2.8}\times10^{-3.5}
      \approx1.67\times3.16\times10^{-4}
      \approx5.3\times10^{-4}\ \text{mol L}^{-1}
\]

Thus the solubility of SrSO₄ is about \(5.3\times10^{-4}\ \text{M}\).

Final answer: \boxed{5.3\times10^{-4}}

The balanced combustion reaction is  

\[
\mathrm{C_3H_8 + 5\,O_2 \rightarrow 3\,CO_2 + 4\,H_2O}
\]

One mole (or one volume) of propane produces three moles (or three volumes) of CO₂ under identical temperature and pressure. Hence  

\[
V_{\mathrm{CO_2}} = 3 \times V_{\mathrm{C_3H_8}} = 3 \times 2500\ \text{L}=7500\ \text{L}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{7500\ \text{L}}

Using Boyle’s law \(P_1V_1=P_2V_2\):

\[
V_2=\frac{P_1V_1}{P_2}= \frac{28.0\ \text{atm}\times 12.0\ \text{L}}{9.70\ \text{atm}}
      =\frac{336}{9.70}\approx 34.6\ \text{L}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{34.6\ \text{L}}

Using Charles’s law \(\displaystyle \frac{V_1}{T_1}=\frac{V_2}{T_2}\) with temperatures in kelvin:

\(T_1 =55.0^{\circ}\mathrm{C}+273.15 = 328.15\ \mathrm{K}\)  
\(T_2 =30.0^{\circ}\mathrm{C}+273.15 = 303.15\ \mathrm{K}\)

\(V_2 = V_1\frac{T_2}{T_1}=3.60\;\text{L}\times\frac{303.15}{328.15}
\approx 3.33\;\text{L}\).

Final answer: \boxed{3.33\ \text{L}}

The amount‑of‑substance unit mol is defined such that one mole contains Avogadro’s number of entities. Thus  

\[
1.00\ \text{mol}=6.022\times10^{23}\ \text{entities}
\]

(rounded to three significant figures to match the 1.00 mol precision).  

Final answer: \boxed{6.022\times10^{23}}

pOH = 14 – 12.4 = 1.6  

\[
[\mathrm{OH^-}] = 10^{-\,\text{pOH}} = 10^{-1.6}=0.0251\ \text{M}
\]

Moles of OH⁻ needed in 3.55 L:

\[
n = 0.0251\ \text{mol L}^{-1}\times 3.55\ \text{L}=0.0891\ \text{mol}
\]

Volume of 0.855 M KOH to provide this amount:

\[
V = \frac{n}{C}= \frac{0.0891\ \text{mol}}{0.855\ \text{mol L}^{-1}}=0.104\ \text{L}
\]

\[
V \approx 1.04\times10^{2}\ \text{mL}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{1.04\times10^{2}\ \text{mL}}

Using \(C_1V_1=C_2V_2\):

\(C_2=\dfrac{C_1V_1}{V_2}= \dfrac{(3.0\times10^{-5})(40.0\ \text{mL})}{100\ \text{mL}} =3.0\times10^{-5}\times0.40 =1.2\times10^{-5}\).

Final answer: \boxed{1.2\times10^{-5}}

The milliequivalent concentration is  

\[
\text{mEq/L}= \frac{\text{mass (g)}}{\text{atomic weight (g·mol}^{-1})}\times\text{valence}\times\frac{1000}{\text{volume (L)}}
\]

For Mg\(^{2+}\): atomic weight ≈ 24.305 g·mol\(^{-1}\), valence = 2, mass = 0.600 g, volume = 1.930 L.

\[
\text{mEq/L}= \frac{0.600}{24.305}\times2\times\frac{1000}{1.930}
   =0.02470\times2\times\frac{1000}{1.930}
   =0.04940\times\frac{1000}{1.930}
   \approx 25.6\ \text{mEq/L}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{25.6\ \text{mEq/L}}

The reduction half‑reaction involves oxygen gaining electrons to form oxide ions:

\[
\mathrm{O_2 + 4\,e^- \;\longrightarrow\; 2\,O^{2-}}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{\mathrm{O_2 + 4\,e^- \;\rightarrow\; 2\,O^{2-}}}

At STP one mole of any ideal gas occupies \(22.4\ \text{L}\).  

Moles in \(560\ \text{mL}=0.560\ \text{L}\):
\[
n=\frac{0.560\ \text{L}}{22.4\ \text{L mol}^{-1}}=0.025\ \text{mol}
\]

Molecular mass \(M\) is mass divided by moles:
\[
M=\frac{1.10\ \text{g}}{0.025\ \text{mol}}=44\ \text{g mol}^{-1}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{44\ \text{g mol}^{-1}}

One formula unit of AlCl₃ contains one aluminum atom and three chlorine atoms, for a total of  

\(1 + 3 = 4\) atoms.

Final answer: \boxed{4}

The total pressure is the sum of the partial pressures:

\[
P_{\text{total}} = 1.61\times10^{4}\ \text{kPa}+4.34\times10^{5}\ \text{kPa}
                = 0.161\times10^{5}\ \text{kPa}+4.34\times10^{5}\ \text{kPa}
                = 4.501\times10^{5}\ \text{kPa}
\]

Rounded to three significant figures (the precision of the given data):

\[
P_{\text{total}}\approx 4.50\times10^{5}\ \text{kPa}.
\]

Final answer: \boxed{4.50 \times 10^{5}\ \text{kPa}}

The number of moles is \[
n = \frac{m}{M}
\] where \(m = 80\text{ g}\) and the molar mass of sulfur \(M \approx 32.07\text{ g mol}^{-1}\).

\[
n = \frac{80\text{ g}}{32.07\text{ g mol}^{-1}} = 2.49\text{ mol}
\]

Rounded to two significant figures, this is about \(2.5\) moles.

Final answer: \boxed{2.5}

The sulfate ion is represented by the chemical formula  

\[
\mathrm{SO_4^{2-}}
\]

which indicates a central sulfur atom tetrahedrally coordinated to four oxygen atoms and carrying an overall charge of \(-2\).

Final answer: \boxed{\mathrm{SO_4^{2-}}}

The specific heat \(c\) is obtained from \(Q = m c \Delta T\).

\[
\Delta T = 25.0^\circ\text{C} - 50.4^\circ\text{C} = -25.4^\circ\text{C}
\]
\[
|c| = \frac{|Q|}{m\,|\Delta T|} = \frac{114\ \text{J}}{10.0\ \text{g}\times 25.4^\circ\text{C}}
       = \frac{114}{254}\ \text{J g}^{-1}\!^\circ\text{C}^{-1}
       \approx 0.449\ \text{J g}^{-1}\!^\circ\text{C}^{-1}
\]

In SI units this is \(0.449\ \text{J g}^{-1}\!^\circ\text{C}^{-1}\times 10^{3}=449\ \text{J kg}^{-1}\! \text{K}^{-1}\).

Final answer: \boxed{0.449\ \text{J g}^{-1}\!^\circ\text{C}^{-1}}

Using the ideal‑gas law \(PV=nRT\),

\[
P=\frac{913\ \text{mmHg}}{760\ \text{mmHg atm}^{-1}}=1.201\ \text{atm}
\]

\[
V=\frac{nRT}{P}= \frac{(0.963\ \text{mol})(0.08206\ \text{L·atm·K}^{-1}\text{mol}^{-1})(300.9\ \text{K})}{1.201\ \text{atm}}
\]

\[
V\approx\frac{23.78\ \text{L·atm}}{1.201\ \text{atm}} \approx 19.8\ \text{L}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{19.8\ \text{L}}

Cancel the ions that appear unchanged on both sides (the chloride and sodium ions). The remaining species give the net ionic equation:

\[
2\,\mathrm{Fe^{3+}(aq)} + 3\,\mathrm{CO_3^{2-}(aq)} \longrightarrow \mathrm{Fe_2(CO_3)_3(s)}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{2Fe^{3+}(aq) + 3CO_3^{2-}(aq) \rightarrow Fe_2(CO_3)_3(s)}

At STP one mole of an ideal gas occupies \(22.4\ \text{L}\).  
Moles of \(\mathrm{CO_2}\) = \(\dfrac{5.6\ \text{L}}{22.4\ \text{L mol}^{-1}} = 0.25\ \text{mol}\).

Final answer: \boxed{0.25\text{ mol}}

The combustion reaction is  

\[
2\,\mathrm{C_8H_{18}} + 25\,\mathrm{O_2} \;\longrightarrow\; 16\,\mathrm{CO_2} + 18\,\mathrm{H_2O}
\]

Balanced by matching 16 carbon atoms, 36 hydrogen atoms (as 18 water molecules), and 50 oxygen atoms (as 25 O₂).  

Final answer: \boxed{2\,\mathrm{C_8H_{18}} + 25\,\mathrm{O_2} \rightarrow 16\,\mathrm{CO_2} + 18\,\mathrm{H_2O}}

Moles \(n=\dfrac{m}{M}\).  

Molar mass of \(\mathrm{CO_{2}}\):  

\[
M=12.01\;\text{g mol}^{-1}+2(16.00\;\text{g mol}^{-1})=44.01\;\text{g mol}^{-1}
\]

\[
n=\frac{44\ \text{g}}{44.01\ \text{g mol}^{-1}}=0.9998\ \text{mol}\approx 1.0\ \text{mol}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{1.0\ \text{mol}}

The mole fraction \(X_{\text{MeOH}}\) is the ratio of methanol moles to the total moles in the solution:

\[
X_{\text{MeOH}} = \frac{n_{\text{MeOH}}}{n_{\text{MeOH}} + n_{\text{H_2O}}}
               = \frac{6.0\ \text{mol}}{6.0\ \text{mol} + 3.0\ \text{mol}}
               = \frac{6.0}{9.0}
               = 0.667.
\]

Final answer: \boxed{0.667}

Propanes molecular formula is \(C_3H_8\). It contains three carbon atoms and eight hydrogen atoms.  
Total atoms = \(3 + 8 = 11\).

Final answer: \boxed{11}

Using \( \frac{P_{1}V_{1}}{T_{1}}=\frac{P_{2}V_{2}}{T_{2}} \) for a fixed amount of gas:

\[
T_{2}=T_{1}\frac{P_{2}V_{2}}{P_{1}V_{1}}
      =298\text{ K}\times\frac{830\ \text{torr}\times 420\ \text{L}}
                              {350\ \text{torr}\times 450\ \text{L}}
      \approx298\text{ K}\times2.213\approx660\text{ K}.
\]

In Celsius this is \(660-273\approx387^{\circ}\text{C}\).

Final answer: \boxed{660\text{ K (≈387^{\circ}C)}}

Using Boyle’s law \(P_1V_1 = P_2V_2\) (temperature constant),

\[
V_2 = \frac{P_1V_1}{P_2}= \frac{380\ \text{torr}\times 12\ \text{L}}{760\ \text{torr}}
      = \frac{1}{2}\times12\ \text{L}=6\ \text{L}.
\]

Final answer: \boxed{6\ \text{L}}

Using Boyle’s law \(P_1V_1=P_2V_2\) (temperature constant),

\[
V_2=\frac{P_1V_1}{P_2}= \frac{760\ \text{mmHg}\times 1.40\times10^{3}\ \text{mL}}{380\ \text{mmHg}}
      =2\times1.40\times10^{3}\ \text{mL}=2.80\times10^{3}\ \text{mL}.
\]

Final answer: \boxed{2.80\times10^{3}\ \text{mL}}

Molar mass of glucose \(C_6H_{12}O_6\):

\[
M = 6(12.01) + 12(1.008) + 6(16.00) = 180.16\ \text{g·mol}^{-1}
\]

Moles \(n\) from 120 g:

\[
n = \frac{120\ \text{g}}{180.16\ \text{g·mol}^{-1}} \approx 0.666\ \text{mol}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{0.67\ \text{mol}}

Molar mass of nitrous oxide:
\[
M_{\mathrm{N_2O}} = 2(14.0\;\text{g mol}^{-1}) + 16.0\;\text{g mol}^{-1}=44.0\;\text{g mol}^{-1}
\]

Moles of N₂O in 85.0 g:
\[
n_{\mathrm{N_2O}}=\frac{85.0\;\text{g}}{44.0\;\text{g mol}^{-1}}=1.93\;\text{mol}
\]

Each N₂O molecule contains two nitrogen atoms, so the moles of nitrogen atoms are:
\[
n_{\mathrm{N}} = 2 \times 1.93\;\text{mol}=3.86\;\text{mol}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{3.86}

Using Boyle’s law \(P_1V_1=P_2V_2\):

\(V_2 = \dfrac{P_1V_1}{P_2}= \dfrac{28.0\;\text{atm}\times 12.0\;\text{L}}{9.70\;\text{atm}} = \dfrac{336}{9.70}\;\text{L}\approx 34.6\;\text{L}\).

Final answer: \boxed{34.6\text{ L}}

Molarity \(M\) is defined as moles of solute divided by volume of solution in litres:

\[
M=\frac{n}{V_{\text{L}}}
\]

Here \(n = 0.060\ \text{mol}\) and the volume is \(1500\ \text{mL}=1.500\ \text{L}\).

\[
M=\frac{0.060\ \text{mol}}{1.500\ \text{L}}=0.040\ \text{mol·L}^{-1}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{0.040\ \text{M}}

The heat released per mole is \(-1110.0\ \text{kJ mol}^{-1}\). Required moles:

\[
n=\frac{834.20\ \text{kJ}}{1110.0\ \text{kJ mol}^{-1}}=0.7515\ \text{mol}
\]

Mass needed:

\[
m=n \times M =0.7515\ \text{mol}\times 89.45\ \text{g mol}^{-1}=6.722\times10^{1}\ \text{g}\approx 67.2\ \text{g}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{67.2\ \text{g}}

The combustion releases \(1220\ \text{kJ}\) per mole.  
Moles needed for \(649.90\ \text{kJ}\):

\[
n=\frac{649.90\ \text{kJ}}{1220\ \text{kJ mol}^{-1}}=0.5322\ \text{mol}
\]

Mass required:

\[
m=n \times M =0.5322\ \text{mol}\times78.35\ \text{g mol}^{-1}=41.7\ \text{g}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{41.7\ \text{g}}

The number of moles \(n\) is obtained by dividing the number of atoms \(N\) by Avogadro’s constant \(N_A = 6.022\times10^{23}\ \text{atoms mol}^{-1}\):

\[
n = \frac{N}{N_A}= \frac{1.35\times10^{24}\ \text{atoms}}{6.022\times10^{23}\ \text{atoms mol}^{-1}}
   = \frac{1.35}{6.022}\times10^{24-23}
   \approx 0.2242\times10^{1}
   \approx 2.24\ \text{mol}.
\]

Final answer: \boxed{2.24\ \text{mol}}

Moles of nitrogen:  

\[
n_{\text{N}_2}= \frac{50.0\ \text{g}}{28.014\ \text{g·mol}^{-1}} \approx 1.785\ \text{mol}
\]

From the balanced equation \( \mathrm{N_2+3H_2\to2NH_3} \), 3 mol of \( \mathrm{H_2} \) react with 1 mol of \( \mathrm{N_2} \):

\[
n_{\text{H}_2}=3 \times 1.785\ \text{mol}=5.355\ \text{mol}
\]

Mass of hydrogen required:

\[
m_{\text{H}_2}=5.355\ \text{mol}\times 2.016\ \text{g·mol}^{-1}\approx 10.8\ \text{g}
\]

Final answer: \[
\text{Hydrogen needed}=10.8\ \text{g}
\]

Final answer: \boxed{10.8\ \text{g}}

The temperature rise is found from \(Q=m c \Delta T\).

\[
\Delta T=\frac{Q}{m c}= \frac{100.00\ \text{J}}{20.0\ \text{g}\times4.184\ \text{J g}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1}}
          =\frac{100.00}{83.68}=1.20\ \text{K}
\]

Initial temperature \(T_i=30.0^{\circ}\text{C}\).  

\[
T_f = T_i+\Delta T = 30.0^{\circ}\text{C}+1.20^{\circ}\text{C}=31.2^{\circ}\text{C}
\]

Final answer: Final answer: \boxed{31.2^{\circ}\text{C}}

Balancing gives: \(\text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3 + 3\,\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4 \rightarrow \text{Fe}_2(\text{SO}_4)_3 + 3\,\text{H}_2\text{O}\).  
The coefficient of \(\text{H}_2\text{O}\) is \(3\).

Final answer: \boxed{3}

Convert all quantities to SI‑compatible units, then use the ideal‑gas law to find the amount of substance and divide the sample mass by that amount.

\[
P=\frac{749\ \text{mmHg}}{760\ \text{mmHg/atm}}=0.987\ \text{atm}
\]
\[
V=117\ \text{mL}=0.117\ \text{L}
\]
\[
T=95^{\circ}\mathrm{C}=95+273=368\ \text{K}
\]

Ideal‑gas equation \(PV=nRT\) gives  

\[
n=\frac{PV}{RT}
   =\frac{(0.987\ \text{atm})(0.117\ \text{L})}
          {(0.08206\ \text{L·atm·K}^{-1}\text{mol}^{-1})(368\ \text{K})}
   \approx 3.83\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}
\]

Molar mass \(M\) is mass divided by moles:

\[
M=\frac{0.565\ \text{g}}{3.83\times10^{-3}\ \text{mol}}
  \approx 1.48\times10^{2}\ \text{g·mol}^{-1}
\]

Thus the gas has a molar mass of about \(1.5\times10^{2}\,\text{g·mol}^{-1}\).

Final answer: \boxed{148\ \text{g·mol}^{-1}}

The decomposition of silver(I) oxide is  

\[
2\;\text{Ag}_{2}\text{O}\; \rightarrow\; 4\;\text{Ag}\;+\;\text{O}_{2}
\]

