Iodic acid ( HIO₃ ) is a strong oxidizing agent. In acidic solution the iodate ion \(\mathrm{IO_3^-}\) can accept six electrons, while the iodide ion \(\mathrm{I^-}\) can donate one electron each to form molecular iodine.

**Half‑reactions**

Reduction (iodate to iodide)  
\[
\mathrm{IO_3^- + 6 H^+ + 6 e^- \;\longrightarrow\; I^- + 3 H_2O}
\]

Oxidation (iodide to iodine)  
\[
\mathrm{2 I^- \;\longrightarrow\; I_2 + 2 e^-}
\]

Multiply the oxidation half‑reaction by 3 so that the electrons cancel with the reduction half‑reaction, then add:

\[
\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{IO_3^- + 6 H^+ + 6 e^-} &\longrightarrow \mathrm{I^- + 3 H_2O}\\
3\bigl(\mathrm{2 I^- \;\longrightarrow\; I_2 + 2 e^-}\bigr) &\longrightarrow \mathrm{6 I^- \;\longrightarrow\; 3 I_2 + 6 e^-}
\end{aligned}
\]

Cancel the \(6e^-\) and combine the remaining species:

\[
\boxed{\mathrm{IO_3^- + 5 I^- + 6 H^+ \;\longrightarrow\; 3 I_2 + 3 H_2O}}
\]

Thus, iodic acid oxidizes iodide to elemental iodine, producing water as the only other product.  

Final answer: \boxed{\mathrm{IO_3^- + 5 I^- + 6 H^+ \;\rightarrow\; 3 I_2 + 3 H_2O}}

